    slug:"probability-statistics", title:"Probability & Statistics — JEE Maths Lesson | vlymbooq",
    desc:"Learn JEE Probability & Statistics simply. Mean, median, variance, probability, Bayes theorem, binomial distribution from basics.",
    tags:"Mean &sdot; Median &sdot; Variance &sdot; Probability &sdot; Bayes &sdot; Binomial &sdot; Correlation",
    theory:'<h3>Let\'s Understand Probability & Statistics — Super Simple!</h3>\n\n<h4>What is Statistics?</h4>\n<p>Statistics is about <strong>understanding data</strong>. If you have test scores of a class, statistics helps you answer: "What\'s the average? How spread out are the scores? What\'s the typical score?"</p>\n\n<h4>1. Mean, Median, Mode</h4>\n<p><strong>Mean (average):</strong> Add all numbers, divide by how many there are. (2+4+6+8+10)/5 = 6. The "center of mass".</p>\n<p><strong>Median:</strong> Sort the numbers, pick the middle one. 2,4,6,8,10 → median = 6 (third number). If even count, average the two middle numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>Mode:</strong> The number that appears most often. 1,2,2,3,4 → mode = 2.</p>\n\n<div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">Example: Mean</div><div class="ex-text">Find mean of 2,4,6,8,10.</div><div class="ex-soln">(2+4+6+8+10)/5 = 30/5 = 6.</div></div>\n\n<h4>2. Variance and Standard Deviation</h4>\n<p><strong>Variance</strong> measures how spread out the numbers are — the average of squared differences from the mean. σ² = Σ(x-x̄)²/n.</p>\n<p><strong>Standard deviation</strong> σ = √variance. If σ is small → numbers are close together. If σ is large → numbers are spread out.</p>\n<p><strong>Key fact:</strong> Adding 5 to every number increases the mean by 5 but <strong>variance stays same</strong> (spread doesn\'t change). Multiplying every number by k multiplies mean by k and variance by k².</p>\n<p><strong>Combined mean</strong> of two groups: x̄ = (n₁x̄₁ + n₂x̄₂)/(n₁+n₂).</p>\n\n<div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">Example: Variance</div><div class="ex-text">Find variance of 2,2,2,2,2.</div><div class="ex-soln">All values are equal → no spread → variance = 0.</div></div>\n\n<h4>3. What is Probability?</h4>\n<p><strong>Probability</strong> = (favorable outcomes) / (total possible outcomes). Range: 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain).</p>\n<p>P(heads on coin) = 1/2. P(rolling a 4 on die) = 1/6. P(ace from deck) = 4/52 = 1/13.</p>\n<p><strong>Addition rule:</strong> P(A∪B) = P(A)+P(B)-P(A∩B). For mutually exclusive (can\'t happen together) → P(A∪B) = P(A)+P(B).</p>\n<p><strong>Multiplication rule:</strong> P(A∩B) = P(A)×P(B) for independent events. If dependent: P(A∩B) = P(A)×P(B|A).</p>\n\n<div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">Example: Probability</div><div class="ex-text">Find P(at least 1 head in 2 coin tosses).</div><div class="ex-soln">1 - P(TT) = 1 - 1/4 = 3/4. Using complement is easier!</div></div>\n\n<h4>4. Conditional Probability</h4>\n<p>P(A|B) = P(A∩B)/P(B). The "|" means "given that". So P(A|B) = "probability of A, assuming B already happened".</p>\n<p>Example: P(rain | cloudy) is higher than P(rain) because we already know it\'s cloudy.</p>\n<p><strong>Independent events</strong>: P(A∩B) = P(A)×P(B). If A and B are independent, knowing B happened tells us nothing about A.</p>\n\n<h4>5. Bayes Theorem — Reversing Probability</h4>\n<p>Bayes theorem lets us <strong>reverse</strong> conditional probability: P(A|B) = P(B|A)×P(A)/P(B).</p>\n<p>Think of it like: If you see wet ground (B), what\'s the chance it rained (A)? You know P(wet | rain) is high, but you want P(rain | wet). Bayes flips it!</p>\n\n<h4>6. Random Variables and Distributions</h4>\n<p>A <strong>random variable</strong> assigns numbers to outcomes. Like: X = number of heads in 3 coin tosses. X can be 0, 1, 2, or 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Probability distribution:</strong> A table showing each value and its probability. Sum of all probabilities = 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Binomial distribution:</strong> For n independent trials, each with success probability p. P(X=r) = ⁿC<sub>r</sub> p<sup>r</sup> q<sup>n-r</sup> (q=1-p). Mean = np. Variance = npq.</p>\n\n<div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">Example: Binomial Mean</div><div class="ex-text">Mean of Binomial B(10, 0.3).</div><div class="ex-soln">Mean = np = 10×0.3 = 3.</div></div>\n\n<h4>7. Correlation and Regression</h4>\n<p><strong>Correlation</strong> measures how two variables move together. r = 1 → perfect positive. r = -1 → perfect negative. r = 0 → no relationship.</p>\n<p><strong>Regression</strong> finds the line of best fit: y = a + bx. b = covariance/variance of x. a = ȳ - bx̄.</p>',
    derivations:'<div class="derivation-box"><div class="dv-title">Derivation 1: Bayes Theorem — How to Reverse Probability</div><div class="dv-content"><span class="step">Step 1:</span> By definition: P(A|B) = P(A∩B)/P(B). (Eq. 1)<br><span class="step">Step 2:</span> Also: P(B|A) = P(A∩B)/P(A). (Eq. 2)<br><span class="step">Step 3:</span> From Eq. 2: P(A∩B) = P(B|A)×P(A).<br><span class="step">Step 4:</span> Substitute into Eq. 1: P(A|B) = P(B|A)×P(A)/P(B).<br><span class="step">Step 5:</span> P(B) can be expanded using total probability if needed. That\'s Bayes theorem — it lets us "reverse" the conditional probability.</div></div><div class="derivation-box"><div class="dv-title">Derivation 2: Mean of Binomial Distribution = np</div><div class="dv-content"><span class="step">Step 1:</span> For Binomial: P(X=r) = <sup>n</sup>C<sub>r</sub>p<sup>r</sup>q<sup>n-r</sup>, where q=1-p.<br><span class="step">Step 2:</span> Mean E(X) = Σ r·P(X=r) = Σ r·<sup>n</sup>C<sub>r</sub>p<sup>r</sup>q<sup>n-r</sup>.<br><span class="step">Step 3:</span> Key trick: r·<sup>n</sup>C<sub>r</sub> = n·<sup>n-1</sup>C<sub>r-1</sub>. (Try it with numbers to see why!)<br><span class="step">Step 4:</span> So E(X) = n·Σ <sup>n-1</sup>C<sub>r-1</sub>p<sup>r</sup>q<sup>n-r</sup> = np·Σ <sup>n-1</sup>C<sub>s</sub>p<sup>s</sup>q<sup>n-1-s</sup> (letting s=r-1).<br><span class="step">Step 5:</span> The sum is just (p+q)<sup>n-1</sup> = 1<sup>n-1</sup> = 1. Therefore E(X) = np.</div></div><div class="derivation-box"><div class="dv-title">Derivation 3: Variance = E(X²) - [E(X)]²</div><div class="dv-content"><span class="step">Step 1:</span> By definition: Var(X) = E[(X - μ)²] where μ = E(X).<br><span class="step">Step 2:</span> Expand: (X - μ)² = X² - 2μX + μ².<br><span class="step">Step 3:</span> Take expectation: E[(X - μ)²] = E(X²) - 2μE(X) + μ².<br><span class="step">Step 4:</span> But E(X) = μ, so: = E(X²) - 2μ² + μ² = E(X²) - μ².<br><span class="step">Step 5:</span> Therefore: Var(X) = E(X²) - [E(X)]². This form is often easier to compute!</div></div><div class="derivation-box"><div class="dv-title">Derivation 4: Why Is Variance Unchanged by Adding Constant?</div><div class="dv-content"><span class="step">Step 1:</span> Let Y = X + c, where c is a constant.<br><span class="step">Step 2:</span> E(Y) = E(X) + c. (Mean shifts by c.)<br><span class="step">Step 3:</span> Var(Y) = E[(Y - E(Y))²] = E[(X + c - (E(X)+c))²].<br><span class="step">Step 4:</span> The c cancels! = E[(X - E(X))²] = Var(X).<br><span class="step">Step 5:</span> So adding a constant shifts the center but doesn\'t change spread. Intuitive: if everyone gets +5 marks, the spread stays identical.</div></div><div class="tip-box"><div class="tip-title">JEE Shortcut 1</div><div class="tip-text">Adding a constant changes mean but NOT variance. Multiplying by k multiplies mean by k and variance by k². For "at least one" probability problems, always try complement: P(at least one) = 1 - P(none).</div></div><div class="tip-box"><div class="tip-title">JEE Shortcut 2</div><div class="tip-text">Variance of binomial = npq. SD = √(npq). For Bayes problems, draw a tree diagram. First branch: P(A). Second branch: P(B|A) and P(B|A\').</div></div><div class="tip-box"><div class="tip-title">JEE Shortcut 3</div><div class="tip-text">P(A∪B) = P(A)+P(B) only if A and B are mutually exclusive. If not, subtract P(A∩B). For "either A or B but not both": P(A)+P(B)-2P(A∩B).</div></div>',
